Choose a SIMPLE IRA if you have 100 or fewer employees, want little paperwork, and can fund the required annual employer contribution through a 3% match (which can drop to 1% in up to 2 of 5 years with notice) or a 2% nonelective contribution. Choose a 401(k) for higher employee savings caps, vesting schedules, loan access, and the flexibility to skip employer matching in a lean year. A 401(k) offers the highest total limits and plan flexibility for top earners, though neither plan suits money needed before 59½, and withdrawals during your first 2 years of a SIMPLE IRA cost 25% instead of 10%.